From 06a86c193f52cd54ae81010d79957b9b649f0310 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: daveey <daveey@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sat, 14 Nov 2026 17:56:26 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] [daveey] shell_arith_sink: a `let` command word hidden by
 quotes or escapes is still `let`
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Bash removes quotes before it looks the command word up, so `\let`, `'let'`, `l\et`,
`l'e't`, `let''`, `"le"t`, `$'\x6cet'`, `$'\554et'` and `$"let"` all run the `let`
builtin, whose operands are arithmetic: `\let 'a[$(f)]=1'` runs `f`. The scanner's let
token needed the bare literal word, so every such spelling hid the sink
(t_db248629e705196f, found reviewing #3062).

Fix: at a quote or backslash-escape token in command context, `tools/shell_let_word.py`
decodes the word it starts (letters, escapes, line continuations, '…', "…", $'…' with
bash's ANSI-C escapes, $"…") and, when it spells `let` followed by a blank, the scanner
opens the same `let` arithmetic frame as the bare token. Like the bare token this ignores
command position, so `echo \let 'a[$(f)]'` also counts; that only widens the walk (fails
closed), exactly as `echo let 'a[$(f)]'` already did.

Scope decisions:
- B (`unset`/`printf -v`/`[[ -v ]]`/`test -v`/`declare`/`typeset`/`local`/`read` name
  operands) belongs to PR #2788, which adds builtin-target frames to this scanner.
  Its current head catches the double-quoted forms; the apostrophe, ANSI-C and escaped
  forms are reported there (PR comment) and tracked by follow-up card t_8e93020e33282e3d.
- C (`b='['; let "a${b}"'$(f)]=1'`, `x='a[$(f)]'; let 'x'`, `"le$(echo t)"`) needs data
  flow, which this static tokenizer does not model. Out of scope; recorded on the card.

Proof:
- New tests/cron/test_lifecycle_guard_arith_let_word.py (73 tests): 31 real-Bash marker
  sinks (marker touched; scanner and output_reaches_shell must flag), 8 real-Bash data
  controls (marker not touched; scanner must not flag), 3 double-quoted controls whose
  substitution output bash prints rather than evaluates, lifecycle-guard scans of the
  hidden-let payload, and word-decoding unit cases. RED on origin/main 2e2817ca: 33
  failures (27 sinks + 6 lifecycle-guard scans). GREEN with the fix.
- test_lifecycle_guard_arith_word_quotes.py unchanged and green (its DATA list does not
  flip). All 43 test files that import the arith scanner, shell_heredoc or the lifecycle
  guard: 2923 passed.
- Mutants (sdlc-review mutants.py, 19 run): 15 killed. The 4 survivors are equivalent
  bounds that only stop early (token filter, prefix stop, two span limits): a word that
  is longer or holds other characters can never decode to `let`.
- The escape / double-quote handling moved with the new check into `_word_token`, so
  `substitution_in_arithmetic` keeps its complexity cap (scripts/check: 20 checks ok).
- Linear: 40k repetitions of escape/quote-heavy units (≤320 KB) scan in ≤0.14 s, matching
  the parent's timings.
